<para>Do you notice a pattern here? 10,000 is \( \frac{50,000}{5} \), 5,000 is \( \frac{50,000}{10} \), and 2,500 is
  \( \frac{50,000}{20} \).</para>
<para>We can generalize that the amount each organization receives is 50,000 divided by the number of selected
  organizations, or \( d=\frac{50,000}{n} \).</para>
<title>Try It: Finding Relationships between Quantities</title>
<para>A local business is going to hand out $20,000 in scholarships to students at local high schools.</para>
<para>What is the relationship between the number of students, \( s \), and the dollar amount each student will receive, \(
  d \)?</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="4ae6ad3d-d3bf-4687-9109-13c3606729bf" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
